over via a transient file \(en never argv, never env)
.TP
\f[B]stage attest <stage>\f[R]
Detach\-sign the stage manifest with the bound attest key: gpg is there,
the stage exists, the attest\-key is bound and complete, the manifest is
there; then `stage detachsign' (armored detached signature
boot/manifest.asc, exactly what the loader verifies against the anchor)
.TP
\f[B]stage checkout <stage> <ref>\f[R]
Add a DETACHED git worktree of ELEBAKE_FREEBSD_SRC at <ref> (a snapshot,
never a branch to commit on) and point the stage at it: the toolchain is
there, the stage exists, the source repo is set; then `stage worktree'
.TP
\f[B]stage clean <stage>\f[R]
cleandir the worktree\(cqs stand/ build and reset the stage\(cqs build
outputs: the stage exists and is checked out; then `stage clean dir' and
`stage reset'
.TP
\f[B]stage build <stage>\f[R]
The isolated stand/ build: the toolchain, the build gate (keys bound),
clean, then one `stage build stand <stage> <component>' per curated
component
.TP
\f[B]stage make <stage>\f[R]
BUILD the stage: build (gate, clean, stand), install, include, sign \(en
publishing is stage push.
Needs executing interpreter pins on the terminals underneath, otherwise
every step only displays (see the environment topic)
.TP
\f[B]stage install <stage>\f[R]
Unprivileged stand/ DESTDIR install into destdir/: one `stage install
<stage> <component>' per component of ELEBAKE_STAND_INSTALL_SUBDIRS
(curated policy); an unset list is an error line
.TP
\f[B]stage build kernel <stage>\f[R]
Build the kernel from the stage\(cqs checkout \(en the source delivers
EVERYTHING, the filter selects: the stage exists and is checked out,
ELEBAKE_KERNCONF is set (no implicit default: the guarding kernel is a
decision) and exists in the checkout; then `stage build kernel run'
(isolated per\-stage obj)
.TP
\f[B]stage install kernel <stage>\f[R]
Unprivileged installkernel into the stage\(cqs destdir
(destdir/boot/kernel becomes selectable by the filter): the stage exists
and is checked out, ELEBAKE_KERNCONF is set, the obj tree exists; then
`stage install kernel run'
.TP
\f[B]stage loader <stage> <loader.efi>\f[R]
Ingest an ALREADY\-BUILT external loader as boot/loader.efi (sign it
without building): the stage exists, the source file exists; then `stage
loader ingest'
.TP
\f[B]stage manifest <stage>\f[R]
Generate the REAL veriexec manifest over boot/ (path sha256=hash,
LC_ALL=C\-sorted, every file except the manifest pair; loader.efi
included on purpose \(en it is the disaster reserve, and an unverified
reserve is not a reserve): the stage exists and is populated; then
`stage manifest write' \(en the WHOLE manifest is computed at
generation, the emission is one concrete heredoc write
.TP
\f[B]stage verify <stage>\f[R]
Manifest consistency BOTH ways, checked at generation time: the stage
exists, the manifest is there; then `stage verify listed' (every listed
entry exists and hashes identically) and `stage verify unlisted' (every
file in boot/ is listed)
.TP
\f[B]stage trust <stage>\f[R]
Root of trust into the worktree, in one step: `stage trust anchor'
exports the bound attest key (its public key and a self\-test signature)
into lib/libsecureboot, `stage trust mk' writes site.trust.mk \(en the
veriexec anchor the loader is BUILT with, so this runs after `stage
attest key' and before `stage build'.
Assumes: the attest key is RSA (the loader\(cqs BearSSL verifies RSA
only) and its keyring is readable: the shipped profiles pin the anchor
export to sudo sh (the keyring lives under /root), so the batch asks for
the password by itself
.TP
\f[B]stage trust anchor <stage>\f[R]
Export the attest key + self\-test signature into the worktree\(cqs
libsecureboot: the stage exists and is checked out, the attest key is
bound and complete; then `stage trust anchor export'
.TP
\f[B]stage trust mk <stage>\f[R]
Write site.trust.mk (elevated veriexec config) into the worktree: the
stage exists and is checked out; then `stage trust mk write'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter list <stage>\f[R]
The curated list as it stands: the stage exists; then `stage filter
table'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter uncurated <stage> <srcdir>\f[R]
What the source directory holds at top level that the filter does not
cover: the stage exists, the source is absolute; then `stage filter
uncurated table'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter orphaned <stage>\f[R]
What lies in boot/ that the filter does not cover and no step generates:
the stage exists; then `stage filter orphaned table'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter prune <stage>\f[R]
Emit the removal of every orphaned entry of boot/ (what `stage filter
orphaned' lists: neither curated nor generated) \(en one rm per entry,
displayed by default so the owner reads the list before piping it to sh.
After `stage adopt' this is how a medium\(cqs stale files (BIOS boot
blocks, forth, old loader variants) leave the stage: the curation says
what belongs, prune removes the rest.
Nothing curated or generated is ever touched.
The stage exists; then `stage filter prune rm'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter add <stage> <rel|\->\f[R]
Curate one destdir/boot\-relative path into the boot/ list (idempotent
append); `\-' reads a FROZEN snapshot from stdin, one path per line \(en
a directory entry is a LIVING curation (new files under it travel
along), the snapshot a frozen one.
`\-': rewrite to `stage filter add stdin <stage>', else to `stage filter
add entry <stage> <rel>'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter drop <stage> <rel>\f[R]
Remove one entry from the boot/ curation list: the stage exists, the
entry is listed; then `stage filter remove'
.TP
\f[B]stage filter show <stage> [<srcdir>]\f[R]
ONE view, the whole truth, as three tables: the curated list, what sits
uncurated in the source (default: the stage\(cqs own destdir/boot), what
lies orphaned in boot/
.TP
\f[B]stage include <stage> [<srcdir>]\f[R]
Work the curated filter off from a chosen SOURCE directory (default: the
stage\(cqs own destdir/boot; e.g.\ /boot takes the same selection from
the host\(cqs binaries): the stage exists, the source is absolute and
present, the filter is not empty, every entry is in the source or
already in boot/; then `stage include copy'.
Per entry: present in the source \-> copied into boot/ (directories
replaced whole); absent from the source but present in boot/ \-> kept
and noted (an adopted file the build does not deliver, e.g.\ loader.conf
after stage adopt); absent from both \-> refused before anything ran
.TP
\f[B]stage import <stage> <reldir> [<absfile>]\f[R]
Import ONE base element from another database: two args declare a
record\-tree DIRECTORY, three copy a file/symlink into it \(en an
unconditional check\-then\-act sequence; the logic lives in the dump
that emits these lines.
Three args: the stage exists, the subdir is record\-relative and
declared, the source an absolute existing file or symlink; then `stage
import file'
.TP
\f[B]stage collect [<stage>]\f[R]
Fan out to every stage: one `stage collect <stage>' per name symlink;
none is a comment line
.TP
\f[B]stage adopt <stage> <medium>\f[R]
One\-time adoption of a RUNNING boot tree: copies the medium\(cqs WHOLE
boot/ (cp \-a, existing files in the stage\(cqs boot/ are overwritten)
into the stage \(en boot/ becomes the single source of truth,
configuration (loader.conf & Co.)
included.
A batch: pool import (read\-only), adopt copy, pool export \(en the
export runs even when the copy failed (keep\-going wrapper), so the pool
never stays imported.
Compiled parts the medium brings (kernel, loader, lua, defaults) are
replaced by the stage\(cqs own build the next time `stage include' runs,
so adopt BEFORE include or run include again after it; `stage filter
orphaned' then lists what the medium brought that nothing curates \(en
the owner decides about each such file
.TP
\f[B]stage push <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Publish the stage: manifest, attest, verify, tree onto the medium,
loader onto the ESP
.TP
\f[B]stage dump [<strategy>] [<stage>|all]\f[R]
The stage part of a dump: every stage in the complete vocabulary.
The strategy selects the anchor that knows its blocks (dispatch): `stage
dump complete <stage>' is the full description, `stage dump minimized
<stage>' the rescue subset
.SS Provisioning
The machine\-bound trust expectations: the NVRAM boot marker and the
compiled\-in baselines (local/site.mk).
What turns a generic loader into THIS machine\(cqs tamper detector.
.TP
\f[B]stage baseline add <stage> <MACRO> <digest|string|int> <value>\f[R]
Store one build\-provided value of the stage: a LOADER_TRUST_* macro the
loader sources consume, rendered into site.mk by `stage site mk'
(digest: 64 hex \-> byte list; string: C string; int: bare).
Immutable: an identical re\-add is a no\-op, a differing one is refused
(drop first).
This is where gate slots (gate add) and provider windows get their
values; the dump replays every record
.TP
\f[B]stage baseline drop <stage> <MACRO>\f[R]
Remove one build\-provided value of the stage: the stage and the record
exist, then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]stage baseline show <stage> [<MACRO>]\f[R]
Show the stage\(cqs build\-provided values (or one) as the \-D lines
site mk would render: one `stage baseline show <stage> <MACRO>' per
record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]stage baseline learn <stage> <MACRO> <kenv\-variable>\f[R]
Take a digest baseline from THIS machine\(cqs kenv when the batch runs
\(en the value a trusted boot\(cqs loader published
(loader.trust.<gate>.<leaf>, 64 hex) \(en and record it as `stage
baseline add <stage> <MACRO> digest <value>'.
For the witnesses userland cannot compute (loaded EFI images, ACPI
tables, EFI variables, PCI devices, PCR bank, soft PCR, guarded kenv).
Assumes: the boot that published the value was the owner\(cqs own, on
the intended firmware \(en learning on a tampered platform bakes the
tampering in
.TP
\f[B]stage disks add <stage> <partition>\f[R]
Record one internal GPT partition (nda0p1) whose GELI metadata sector
and whose disk\(cqs partition table the loader measures (measure_geli,
measure_gpt); `stage site mk' hashes them when it runs.
Order of the records = order of hashing.
Assumes: the partitions are GELI providers on GPT disks the firmware
exposes via Block I/O; a legitimate setkey/delkey or repartitioning
moves the digest \(en re\-run site mk afterwards
.TP
\f[B]stage disks drop <stage> <partition>\f[R]
Remove one partition from the stage\(cqs disks record: the stage exists
and lists it, then the line is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage disks show <stage>\f[R]
List the stage\(cqs recorded partitions as notes; none says so
.TP
\f[B]stage conf add <stage> <key> <value>\f[R]
Store one loader.trust.* kenv value of the stage (dumb store, immutable:
identical re\-add is a no\-op, a differing one is refused); `stage
loaderconf mk' writes every record into boot/loader.trust.conf, `stage
require' says which the bound actions demand.
Values that are secrets (hashes) belong here only as HASHES; the medium
carries this file in clear, covered by the manifest
.TP
\f[B]stage conf drop <stage> <key>\f[R]
Remove one loader.trust.* value of the stage: the stage and the record
exist, then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]stage conf show <stage> [<key>]\f[R]
Show the stage\(cqs loader.trust.* values (or one) as the lines
loaderconf mk would write: one `stage conf show <stage> <key>' per
record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]stage require <stage>\f[R]
Report what the stage\(cqs BOUND loader policies demand: the
loader.trust.<gate>.<leaf> keys the actions read at boot (parsed from
the kenv(a, \&...)
calls in the checkout\(cqs action.c, attributed per gate), each with its
conf record or MISSING; then the LOADER_TRUST_* baselines the
measurements and actions consume without a code default, each ok or
MISSING.
With a container argument: the demands of that container\(cqs bound
measurements on the boot tree
.TP
\f[B]stage loaderconf mk <stage>\f[R]
Write boot/loader.trust.conf of the stage from its conf records: the
stage and its checkout exist, boot/loader.conf names the file in
loader_conf_files (stage edit adds it), every leaf a bound action reads
has its value (stage require), then the file is written.
It rides on the medium in clear, the manifest covers it (stage manifest
after this)
.TP
\f[B]stage loaderconf check <stage>\f[R]
Regenerate loader.trust.conf from the conf records and compare with the
stage\(cqs boot/loader.trust.conf: drift per key is reported, agreement
is one line \(en the conf under tamper detection
.TP
\f[B]stage marker record <stage> <BootXXXX> <filepath>\f[R]
Record which load option carries the marker and where its value file
lives (bookkeeping only \(en no NVRAM): the stage exists, the load
option and the absolute path are well\-formed; then `stage marker store'
.TP
\f[B]stage marker write <stage> <restore|new>\f[R]
Write the marker into the recorded load option: `restore' puts the value
from the recorded file back (or generates one when the file is empty
\(en decided at generation time), `new' always generates.
The marker is recorded: rewrite to `stage marker value <restore|new>
<stage>', else an error line
.TP
\f[B]stage marker <stage> <BootXXXX> [<filepath>]\f[R]
Write the boot\-entry marker into that load option: a batch of two \(en
the RECORD (bootvar + value\-file path, bookkeeping, acts) and the WRITE
(NVRAM byte surgery, inspect\-by\-default \(en pipe the output to sudo
sh).
The value comes from the file, or is generated when the file is empty
.TP
\f[B]stage marker rotate <stage>\f[R]
Rotate the marker: a NEW value into the recorded load option and value
file.
The marker is recorded: rewrite to `stage marker write <stage> new'
(inspect\-by\-default, pipe to sudo sh), else an error line
.TP
\f[B]stage site mk <stage>\f[R]
Write local/site.mk of the stage\(cqs checkout: the stage and its
checkout exist; the header, then the measured parts (board, keys,
marker, origin, disks) and the baselines appended in file order to
site.mk.new; the install (rendered, clean, placed) and the report.
Run under sudo: the key store, the marker and the origin are EFI
variables
.TP
\f[B]stage site mk report <stage> [<file>]\f[R]
Show the written site.mk of the stage (default: local/site.mk of its
checkout) as comment lines
.SS Medium
The physical boot medium: bind it, back up its loader, swap, roll back.
.TP
\f[B]stage tree sync <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Write the stage\(cqs boot/ tree onto the medium\(cqs dataset: pool
import, then snapshot + copy + verify (fail\-fast), then close (rollback
if the tree is not the manifest) and pool export \(en close and export
ALWAYS run (keep\-going wrapper), so a failed sync never leaves the pool
imported or the medium half\-written
.TP
\f[B]stage pool import <stage> <medium> <rw|ro>\f[R]
Import the medium\(cqs pool from its GPT label (confined: no device
scan), rooted under $ELEBAKE_ROOT/mnt/<stage>\-<medium>, and mount the
boot dataset: the medium ready, the mode rw or ro, the pool not imported
yet; then `stage pool mount'
.TP
\f[B]stage pool export <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Unmount and export the medium\(cqs pool and remove the altroot: the
medium ready, then `stage pool release' (a pool that is not imported is
a note, not an error, so the closing line of a batch is always safe).
Its pin forces fail\-fast inside, whatever the enclosing batch keeps
going
.TP
\f[B]stage tree snapshot <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Snapshot the medium\(cqs boot dataset (elebake\-<stamp>) before it is
rewritten: the medium ready, the pool imported; then `stage tree snap'
.TP
\f[B]stage tree copy <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Replace the boot/ tree on the MOUNTED dataset with the stage\(cqs boot/:
the medium ready, the stage\(cqs manifest attested, the dataset mounted
and a boot dataset; then `stage tree write'
.TP
\f[B]stage tree verify <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Inspect the boot tree ON the mounted medium against the stage\(cqs
manifest at generation time, both directions: the medium ready, the
manifest there, the dataset mounted; then `stage tree matches' \(en a
finding fails the command
.TP
\f[B]stage tree close <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Leave the medium consistent, decided at generation time: the medium
ready, then `stage tree settle' \(en a tree that IS the manifest stays
(snapshot kept as history), a tree that is not goes back to the newest
elebake snapshot; not imported = nothing to do.
Its pin forces fail\-fast inside, whatever the enclosing batch keeps
going
.TP
\f[B]stage device <stage> <medium> </dev/node> [<mountpoint>]\f[R]
Register a NAMED deploy medium (its backups sort under backup/<medium>):
the stage exists, the medium name and the device node are well\-formed;
then `stage medium record'
.TP
\f[B]stage boot tree <stage> <medium> <gpt\-label> <pool/dataset>\f[R]
Register where the medium\(cqs boot tree lives (used by stage push/tree
sync, adopt, pool import): the stage and the medium exist, the label and
the dataset name are well\-formed, the label \(en when the medium is
inserted \(en names a freebsd\-zfs partition of the medium\(cqs disk,
never its EFI system partition; then `stage boot tree record'
.TP
\f[B]stage backup <stage> <medium> [<label> [<description>]]\f[R]
Save the loader currently ON that medium as a backup RECORD
backup/<medium>/<label>/ (loader.efi, description, sha256, created,
source, by): the stage and the medium exist, the label is a record name
and new (a label is unique per medium and immutable), the description
given, the device present; then `stage backup take'.
Label defaults to the UTC stamp, description to medium/stage/user
.TP
\f[B]stage backup list <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Show the backup records of that medium, oldest first (label, created,
sha256, description \(en the view for a rollback decision): the stage
and the medium exist; then `stage backup table'
.TP
\f[B]stage rollback <stage> <medium> [<label>]\f[R]
Put a backup back onto the medium \(en but FIRST save what is on the
medium now as record suspect\-<stamp> (quietly: the loader about to be
overwritten may be the evidence), then write the named (or newest)
backup and verify its hash on the medium
.TP
\f[B]stage deploy <stage> <medium>\f[R]
The loader swap onto the NAMED medium: the stage and the medium exist,
the device is present, the signed loader is there and newer than the
built one; then `stage deploy write' (mount, backup record of what is
there, copy, hash check, umount)
.SS Trust foundation (catalogs and, soon, claims/gates/policies)
Toolchain readiness checks; each backend answers for itself.
.TP
\f[B]stage measure <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
List the measurements the containers\(cq catalogs offer in the
stage\(cqs checkout, with their descriptions: one `stage measure list
<container> <stage>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, or the one
container given
.TP
\f[B]stage action <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
List the actions the containers\(cq catalogs offer in the stage\(cqs
checkout, with their descriptions: one `stage action list <container>
<stage>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, or the one container given
.TP
\f[B]stage when <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
List the firing predicates the containers\(cq catalogs offer in the
stage\(cqs checkout, with their descriptions: one `stage when list
<container> <stage>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, or the one
container given
.TP
\f[B]catalog doc <file> <name>\f[R]
Print the entry\(cqs name and the description its source carries
(template/awk/catalog\-doc.awk over the catalog file); an entry without
one says so
.TP
\f[B]stage phase policy add <stage> <phase> <policy> [<position>]\f[R]
Bind a policy into a phase of the stage: ensure its prerequisites in the
container that hosts the phase, then append it to the phase\(cqs policy
list (a position inserts instead)
.TP
\f[B]stage phase policy ensure <stage> <phase> <policy>\f[R]
Unroll the containers: one `stage phase policy ensure <container>
<stage> <phase> <policy>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS; the
container that hosts the phase ensures the policy, the others say so in
a comment line
.TP
\f[B]stage policy ensure <stage> <container> <policy>\f[R]
The policy\(cqs prerequisites in the container: every line `<kind>
<name>' of the record (`gate <gate>', `trigger <trigger>') is one `stage
<kind> ensure <stage> <container> <name>'
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation check <stage>\f[R]
The stage exists; every bound policy has its prerequisites (stage phase
policy ensure, per phase and policy); every baseline the bound policies
demand is reported when missing (gate slots, macro expectations, and
what the catalog names demand per template/tbl/baseline\-demands.tbl);
then the summary
.TP
\f[B]stage phase show <stage> [<phase>]\f[R]
List the phases the stage\(cqs checkout offers per container
(ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, in that order), each with its bound policies: one
`stage phase show <container> <stage>' per container; one phase in
detail with the 2\-arg form
.TP
\f[B]stage phase policy drop <stage> <phase> <policy>\f[R]
Unbind a policy from a stage\(cqs phase (the policy itself survives):
the stage exists and the phase binds the policy, then the line is
removed
.TP
\f[B]macro add <MACRO> <type> <label> [<else>] [<defined>]\f[R]
Store a macro record for a compiled baseline: <MACRO> is the C macro
stem (BOARD_DIGEST), <type> sha256 | byte, <label> the measurement
label.
The 4\-arg form names the #else alternative (a verbatim C expression,
`\-' derives MEASUREMENT_NONE), the 5\-arg form also the defined name
(`\-' derives <MACRO> minus _DIGEST plus _EXPECTED).
No arsenal record is referenced: the chain is write only
.TP
\f[B]macro drop <MACRO>\f[R]
Remove a macro record: it exists, no macro expectation names the macro
it defines (a referenced record is never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]macro show [<macro>]\f[R]
Show every macro record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`macro show <macro>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]expectation add <expectation> <type> <label> <value>\f[R]
Store a named, reusable expectation: <type> byte | sha256 | string |
macro; a macro expectation names a macro an arsenal macro record defines
(the reference never dangles; the type word dispatches the check), then
the record is written \(en an identical re\-add is a no\-op, a different
one is refused (immutable; drop first)
.TP
\f[B]expectation drop <expectation>\f[R]
Remove a expectation: it exists, no claim names it as its expectation (a
referenced record is never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]expectation show [<expectation>]\f[R]
Show every expectation record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted:
one `expectation show <expectation>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]claim add <claim> <measurement> <diagnose|\-> <publish|\-> <expectation>\f[R]
Store a named, reusable claim: its expectation must exist in the arsenal
(the reference never dangles), then the record is written \(en an
identical re\-add is a no\-op, a different one is refused (immutable;
drop first)
.TP
\f[B]claim drop <claim>\f[R]
Remove a claim: it exists, no gate lists it (a referenced record is
never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]claim show [<claim>]\f[R]
Show every claim record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`claim show <claim>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]trigger add <trigger> <when> <action>\f[R]
Store a named, reusable FIRE(<when>, &<action>) pair.
when and action are catalog names, checked when a policy binds: no
arsenal record is referenced, the chain is write only \(en an identical
re\-add is a no\-op, a different one is refused (immutable; drop first)
.TP
\f[B]trigger drop <trigger>\f[R]
Remove a trigger: it exists, no policy lists it (a referenced record is
never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]trigger show [<trigger>]\f[R]
Show every trigger record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`trigger show <trigger>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]gate add <gate> [<secret\-slot>] [<duress\-slot>]\f[R]
Create a gate: <gate> is a C identifier (it lands in foundation.c); the
slots are the \-D macros carrying the unlock and duress hashes
(LOADER_TRUST_BOOTLOCK_SECRET, \&..._DURESS), `\-' or absent for none.
No arsenal record is referenced: the chain is write only \(en an
identical re\-add is a no\-op, different slots are refused (immutable;
drop first)
.TP
\f[B]gate drop <gate>\f[R]
Remove a gate: it exists, no policy names it (a referenced record is
never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]gate claim add <gate> <claim> [<position>]\f[R]
Append a claim reference to a gate (order = evaluation order): the gate
and the claim exist (the reference never dangles), then the claim is
appended \(en a claim already listed is a no\-op; the 3\-arg form
inserts at a 1\-based position instead
.TP
\f[B]gate claim drop <gate> <claim>\f[R]
Unlink a claim reference from a gate (the claim itself survives): the
gate exists and lists the claim, then the line is removed
.TP
\f[B]gate show [<gate>]\f[R]
Show every gate record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`gate show <gate>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]policy add <policy> <gate>\f[R]
Create a named policy for a gate (triggers are appended separately): the
gate exists (the reference never dangles), then the record is written
\(en an identical re\-add is a no\-op, a different gate is refused
(immutable; drop first)
.TP
\f[B]policy drop <policy>\f[R]
Remove a policy: it exists, no phase of any stage binds it (a referenced
record is never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]policy trigger add <policy> <trigger> [<position>]\f[R]
Append a trigger (FIRE) reference to a policy: the policy and the
trigger exist (the reference never dangles), then the trigger line is
appended \(en a trigger already listed is a no\-op; the 3\-arg form
inserts at a 1\-based position among the trigger lines instead
.TP
\f[B]policy trigger drop <policy> <trigger>\f[R]
Remove a trigger reference from a policy (the trigger itself survives):
the policy exists and lists the trigger, then the line is removed
.TP
\f[B]policy show [<policy>]\f[R]
Show every policy record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`policy show <policy>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]foundation dump\f[R]
Emit the foundation portion of a database dump: one block per family in
dependency order (macros, expectations, claims, triggers, gates,
policies); adds are idempotent\-immutable, so replays are safe
.TP
\f[B]foundation collect\f[R]
List the arsenal record files (macros, expectations, claims, triggers,
gates, policies) that belong into an archive
.TP
\f[B]answer hash add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <name> <hash> <policy\-template>\f[R]
One sentinel answer class in one batch: the hash is 64 hex digits and
the template policy exists; then expectation <name> (string, label
<loader\-gate>, value <hash> = sha256(salt+word) exactly as the loader
publishes loader.trust.<loader\-gate>.answer), claim <name> over
measure_answer, gate <name> with `\-' and `.' as \(cq_\(cq, policy
<name> with the triggers of the template, and the binding to <phase> of
<stage>.
Assumes the sentinel of <loader\-gate> is bound in the loader
(sentinel_act, leafs question/salt/display) and the container of <phase>
has measure_answer.
Names stay neutral (fish\-3, not fish\-halt): whoever reads the database
must not learn the class from the name
.TP
\f[B]answer add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <name> <policy\-template>\f[R]
Add one sentinel answer class with the word read HIDDEN from the
terminal when the batch runs \(en twice, both must match \(en and hashed
with the stage\(cqs salt (stage conf add <stage>
loader.trust.<loader\-gate>.salt <hex>).
The word reaches neither argv, trace, history, the batch text nor the
generator: the running script leaves only sha256(salt+word) in a file of
the database, the next line lifts it into `answer hash add' and removes
the file.
Write the word down BEFORE typing it (inventory, paper): the database
keeps only the hash
.TP
\f[B]answer drop <stage> <phase> <name>\f[R]
Remove one answer class in reverse order: the binding in <phase>, the
policy, the gate, the claim, the expectation
.TP
\f[B]answer file add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <file>\f[R]
Roll out every answer class of a table file, one line per class `<name>
<policy\-template> <word\&...>' (the word is the rest of the line,
spaces kept; no word = the empty answer; `#' lines and blank lines
skipped): the file is readable by its owner only and the salt exists;
then per class line `answer line hash' (the word hashed when the batch
runs, into .tmp/answer/<name>) and `answer hashed add' (the hash lifted
into `answer hash add') \(en the words reach only the running script,
never argv, trace, the batch text or the generator.
The file IS the written place of the words: keep it on a tmpfs while
typing, encrypt and print it, then remove it (rm \-P)
.TP
\f[B]answer file drop <stage> <phase> <file>\f[R]
Remove every answer class named in a table file (first field of each
class line; the words are not read): one `answer drop' per line
.TP
\f[B]answer catchall add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <name> <policy\-template>\f[R]
The catch\-all answer class: expectation <name> (byte, label
<loader\-gate>, 1), claim over measure_answer_matched, gate, policy with
the triggers of the template \(en a when_fail template (react\-miss:
spool\-fail, shutdown\-fail) \(en and the binding.
It fails whenever no word class matched: empty and wrong alike.
Bind it AFTER the word classes (the phase runs in binding order; a class
added later must be followed by `answer drop' + re\-add of the
catch\-all), and give every class template the trigger for
answer_matched_act
.TP
\f[B]answer show <stage>\f[R]
List the answer classes seen from the stage, in policy order: every
policy whose gate\(cqs first claim measures measure_answer (a word
class) or measure_answer_matched (the catch\-all) \(en name, the phase
of <stage> that binds it or `unbound', its triggers.
The words are not here and never were: the inventory has them
.TP
\f[B]stage earlboot mk <stage>\f[R]
Generate the earlboot rc.d script of the stage into its hooks/earlboot:
the stage and its checkout exist, the bound measurements\(cq demands on
the boot tree are met (stage require <stage> earlboot), then header,
constants, state, tools, the catalog functions, the prologue, the phases
SYSINIT and MOUNTED in that order, the footer, and the install.
Assumes: the record LOADER_TRUST_WORD_SECRET is present when a word
claim or handover is bound; mac_bootlock keeps loader.trust.* immutable;
the script is installed with `stage earlboot install' and covered by
mac_veriexec
.TP
\f[B]stage earlboot test <stage> <dump>\f[R]
Replay a captured boot through the CURRENT earlboot of the stage,
without root and without side effects: the same rendering as `stage
earlboot mk' but with mocks for state and tools \(en kenv/sysctl/kldstat
answer from <dump>, shutdown and logger become notes on stderr, the
state lives under hooks/ and is removed after \(en then the run: exit
code, every file the run left (appraisal, book, spool, marks).
Never run the INSTALLED earlboot by hand: book_act appends to the book.
Capture a dump after a boot with: { kenv; sysctl kern.osrelease
kern.ident kern.securelevel | sed `s/\(ha/sysctl./'; kldstat \-q \-m
mac_bootlock && echo `kldstat.mac_bootlock=1'; } > /tmp/ram/boot.kenv
.TP
\f[B]stage earlboot install <stage>\f[R]
Install the generated earlboot script as /etc/rc.d/earlboot (root:wheel
0500; the root dataset, so the earliest userland does not depend on a
mounted /usr/local) and enable it in /etc/rc.conf.d/earlboot; refused
while none was generated.
Run as root
.TP
\f[B]stage elvbootd mk <stage>\f[R]
Generate the elvbootd hooks of the stage into its hooks/: the stage and
its checkout exist, the bound measurements\(cq demands on the boot tree
are met, at least one runtime phase is bound; then one self\-contained
hook.<phase>.sh per BOUND runtime phase (STARTUP rc.d, PERIODIC
periodic/security, RESUME rc.resume, MEDIA devd with $1 = the cdev),
each with header, constants, state, tools, the catalog functions
(earlboot\(cqs palette inherited), the prologue and its phase; plus the
glue that plugs a hook into its mechanism: hooks/elvbootd (rc.d) when
STARTUP is bound, hooks/elvboot.devd.conf (devd) when MEDIA is bound
.TP
\f[B]stage elvbootd install <stage>\f[R]
Install the generated hooks under /usr/local/etc/elvboot/ (0500) and
wire each into its mechanism with the generated glue: STARTUP as
/usr/local/etc/rc.d/elvbootd (enabled), PERIODIC as
/usr/local/etc/periodic/security/900.elvboot, RESUME as a line in
/etc/rc.resume, MEDIA via /usr/local/etc/devd/elvboot.conf (devd
restarted).
Refused while nothing was generated.
Run as root
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation make <stage>\f[R]
Generate foundation.c from the bound policies into the worktree: the
stage and its checkout exist, the foundation/ directory is there, then
the sections rendered in file order into foundation.c.new (header,
macros, secrets, prerequisites, gates, phases, dispatch) and installed
as one complete file
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation <stage>\f[R]
The foundation batch: check, make, report
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation report <stage>\f[R]
Summarize the foundation of a stage: the checkout, per enum phase its
bound policies, the referenced gates and the emission target \(en notes
for the caller
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All configuration lives in \f[CR]ELEBAKE_*\f[R] variables, layered as
\f[CR].env/local\f[R] (override, written by \f[CR]setenv\f[R]),
\f[CR].env/default\f[R] (the installed profile) and the shipped
templates.
Interpreter pins \f[CR]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_<function>\f[R] decide per
function whether emitted shell is displayed or executed: arity\-specific
before arity\-agnostic before the class default
(\f[CR]ELEBAKE_TERMINAL_INTERPRETER\f[R], default \f[CR]cat\f[R]).
Every variable answers \f[CR]elebake help env <VAR>\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_ARCHIVE_ATTEST_KEY\f[R]
The openpgp record that signs what this database exports \(en and the
signer a receiver expects
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS\f[R]
The containers a stage binds policies into, in this order: loader (the
EFI loader, phases PHASE_BOOT/LOADER/KERNEL, emitted as foundation.c),
earlboot (rc.d at mountcritlocal, SYSINIT/MOUNTED, sh), elvbootd
(runtime hooks, STARTUP/PERIODIC/RESUME/MEDIA, sh).
Each name is a catalog directory under stand/efi/loader/local of the
checkout (the loader in the headers there, the others as
<name>/{policy,measure,action}.sh with a PHASES= line).
A fourth name here is a fourth container: its own catalog directory and
phases, no code change in elebake (JB 08.09.: extensible, an
intermediate layer must be possible)
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_EDITOR\f[R]
The editor \f[CR]stage edit\f[R] opens on a file of the boot tree (stdin
from /dev/tty, so it works under \f[CR]... \(rs| sh\f[R]); edit =
FreeBSD base ee(1).
No fallback to the login environment: an unset pin is a refusal
